@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# RSpec performance notes
+
+Keep measurements scoped to one spec type and one process (`bin/rspec spec/<folder>`). Do not run the full suite or include system/streaming specs in these baselines.
+
+## Model suite - 2026-11-21
+
+Baseline and optimized runs used commit `3748cad`, random RSpec order, the local test database and Redis, and no parallelization.
+
+| Run | Command | RSpec time | Wall time | Result |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| Baseline | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 25` | 131.00s | 133s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Sidekiq policy only | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress` | 91.52s | 93s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Initial shared fixtures | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress` | 83.03s | 85s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+| Final | `bin/rspec spec/models --format progress --seed 5729` | 79.11s | 81s | 1,795 examples, 0 failures |
+
+The final fixed-seed RSpec time is **39.6% lower** than baseline. Wall time is **39.1% lower**. Baseline and final validation both used seed `5729`; the Sidekiq-only and initial shared-fixture runs used `31554` and `19656`, respectively.
+
+### Diagnostic profile
+
+A TestProf pass was run with FactoryProf, RSpecDissect, SQL notifications, and the `sidekiq.inline` event. Profiling instrumentation adds overhead, so these values are attribution data rather than benchmark values.
+
+- 111.50s of 136.28s (81.8%) was spent in setup hooks and memoized fixtures.
+- Inline Sidekiq occupied 37.34s (27.3%) while processing 3,908 jobs.
+- Active Record notifications occupied 21.54s (15.8%) across 113,959 SQL events.
+- FactoryProf counted 4,721 factory calls. `status` (1,003), `account` (1,194), and nested `bookmark` (1,019) creation dominated the total. Times overlap for nested factories and must not be added to suite time.
+- The largest reusable setup costs were repeated status graphs in `TagFeed`, account creation in `Form::Import`, pagination notification sets in `Notification`, and the cleanup-policy status graph in `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy`.
+
+### Model optimizations made
+
+- Model examples use Sidekiq fake mode by default. Examples that must execute an enqueued mail job synchronously use the `:inline_sidekiq` tag. Non-model spec types retain inline mode.
+- `TagFeed`, `Form::Import`, notification pagination, and `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy` now use `let_it_be` for read-mostly shared records. `refind: true` gives every example a fresh Active Record instance while the records themselves are created once inside the enclosing group's transaction.
+- Example assertions, factories, and application runtime behavior were not changed.
+
+Add `:inline_sidekiq` only when an assertion genuinely depends on the worker's synchronous side effect. A job queue assertion should normally continue to use `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` and assert the enqueue itself.
+
+### Next phases
+
+1. Profile `spec/requests` independently before changing request setup. Re-measure Sidekiq/SQL/factory costs and compare like-for-like RSpec times. The model-only Sidekiq policy intentionally does not alter request behavior yet.
+2. Profile factory cascades before editing fabricators. The first candidates are the `status -> bookmark -> account` cascade and unnecessary associated local users/login activity; changes should be opt-in for assertions that require those associations.
+3. Combine the independently measured model and request times for the two-fold overall target. Do not infer suite improvement from a single slow example or a profiled run.
